WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.745
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:09.617
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:16.888
Unit eight: Poetry Analysis. Today you will paint a scene with absolute constructions such as With the sun setting.

4
00:00:17.188 --> 00:00:19.759
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.968
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:25.597
Meter.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:30.858
Meter means the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in a line.

8
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.891
The meter gives the poem a steady pulse.

9
00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:36.660
Your turn. Say: Meter.

10
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.292
Meter.

11
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.016
Imagery.

12
00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:47.419
Imagery means words that create pictures or sensations in the reader's mind.

13
00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:50.097
The imagery of winter fills the poem.

14
00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:53.103
Your turn. Say: Imagery.

15
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:56.921
Imagery.

16
00:00:57.683 --> 00:00:58.696
Metaphor.

17
00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:03.192
Metaphor means a comparison that says one thing is another.

18
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:06.096
Calling time a river is a metaphor.

19
00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:09.162
Your turn. Say: Metaphor.

20
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:12.966
Metaphor.

21
00:01:13.717 --> 00:01:15.089
Stanza structure.

22
00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:19.975
Stanza structure means the way a poem is divided into groups of lines.

23
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:23.593
The stanza structure changes in the final verse.

24
00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:27.416
Your turn. Say: Stanza structure.

25
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:31.871
Stanza structure.

26
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:33.664
Poetic form.

27
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:38.844
Poetic form means the established pattern of a poem, such as a sonnet.

28
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.659
A sonnet is a strict poetic form.

29
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.135
Your turn. Say: Poetic form.

30
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.036
Poetic form.

31
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:50.459
Tone.

32
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.518
Tone means the writer's attitude shown through word choice.

33
00:01:55.117 --> 00:01:57.372
The tone moves from joy to regret.

34
00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.334
Your turn. Say: Tone.

35
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.015
Tone.

36
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.099
Figurative language.

37
00:02:07.083 --> 00:02:11.916
Figurative language means words used for imaginative effect rather than their literal meaning.

38
00:02:12.517 --> 00:02:14.853
Figurative language makes ideas vivid.

39
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.648
Your turn. Say: Figurative language.

40
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.058
Figurative language.

41
00:02:23.817 --> 00:02:24.829
Close reading.

42
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:30.196
Close reading means slow, careful study of the exact words of a text.

43
00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.445
Close reading revealed a hidden pattern of sounds.

44
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.308
Your turn. Say: Close reading.

45
00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:40.400
Close reading.

46
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.677
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:52.267
Visal, a critic describes a poem: 'With the sun setting, the poet turns to memory.' What is 'With the sun setting'?

48
00:02:53.383 --> 00:03:05.451
An absolute construction. With + noun + participle or adjective adds a scene or reason without a full clause: With the village asleep, the speaker is free to remember.

49
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.747
Why is it useful in analysis?

50
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:22.278
It describes imagery briefly and links it to a meaning: The poem ends with the lamp burning low, suggesting fading hope. Its own subject is separate from the main subject.

51
00:03:23.383 --> 00:03:24.686
Any other forms?

52
00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:36.786
Without the with: 'The sun setting, the poet turns to memory.' Both are formal. Do not mix subjects carelessly: the absolute phrase has its own noun.

53
00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:39.422
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.283 --> 00:03:45.917
Grammar focus: Absolute constructions: With plus noun plus participle and adjective.

55
00:03:46.217 --> 00:03:47.462
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.753
With the sun setting, the poet turns to memory.

57
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.602
With the village asleep, the speaker is free to remember.

58
00:03:55.517 --> 00:03:58.805
The final stanza ending in silence, the tone becomes sad.

59
00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:08.340
With + noun + participle / adjective adds a scene or reason. The noun is its own subject, so the clause stays separate from the main subject.

60
00:04:09.083 --> 00:04:12.885
Be careful! Don't say: With the lamp burn low, he wrote.

61
00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:17.274
Say: With the lamp burning low, he wrote.

62
00:04:17.574 --> 00:04:19.469
Use the -ing form after the noun.

63
00:04:20.267 --> 00:04:22.791
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.135
Reading a Poem Closely.

65
00:04:25.435 --> 00:04:29.095
Close reading begins with the exact words and the pattern of the meter.

66
00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:33.783
With the imagery growing darker, the tone shifts from hope to regret.

67
00:04:34.767 --> 00:04:40.087
The poem uses a metaphor of the sea, its figurative language suggesting both danger and freedom.

68
00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.118
With the stanza structure breaking in the final verse, the reader senses a loss of control.

69
00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:52.709
A strict poetic form, such as the sonnet, makes every small change in meter easier to notice.

70
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.709
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:00.933
With the lights blank, the poem ends.

72
00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:04.758
A, fading. B, fade. C, to fade.

73
00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.462
The answer is A: fading.

74
00:05:11.617 --> 00:05:14.731
A comparison that says one thing is another is a blank.

75
00:05:15.031 --> 00:05:18.336
A, meter. B, tone. C, metaphor.

76
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.271
The answer is C: metaphor.

77
00:05:25.417 --> 00:05:27.033
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:27.333 --> 00:05:35.514
A, With the stanza ending, the mood changes.. B, With the stanza end, the mood changes.. C, With the stanza to ends, the mood changes..

79
00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:42.680
The answer is A: With the stanza ending, the mood changes..

80
00:05:43.817 --> 00:05:46.606
The writer's attitude shown through word choice is blank.

81
00:05:46.906 --> 00:05:50.780
A, tone. B, stanza. C, form.

82
00:05:54.333 --> 00:05:56.043
The answer is A: tone.

83
00:05:57.183 --> 00:06:00.147
According to the reading, close reading begins with…

84
00:06:00.447 --> 00:06:05.493
A, the author's life. B, the exact words. C, the ending only.

85
00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:11.660
The answer is B: the exact words.

86
00:06:12.867 --> 00:06:16.100
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:21.295
How does close reading a poem change your understanding of it compared to a first read?

88
00:06:30.083 --> 00:06:31.735
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:32.035 --> 00:06:56.632
A first read gives a general feeling, while close reading shows how that feeling is made. By studying the imagery, meter and tone line by line, I notice patterns that I missed at first, such as a repeated sound or an unexpected metaphor. With each rereading, the meaning grows deeper and I understand why the poet chose each word.

90
00:06:56.932 --> 00:06:58.711
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:59.617 --> 00:07:06.483
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and absolute constructions: with plus noun plus participle and adjective.

92
00:07:06.783 --> 00:07:11.723
Today's value is appreciation of beauty. Learn to notice and value the beauty around you.

93
00:07:12.023 --> 00:07:14.478
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:14.778 --> 00:07:17.988
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

